sentences.l:
Then again, to Tydeus’-son Diomedes, Pallas Athena
gave force and courage, so that plain to see among all
the Argives he might become, and good fame win;

She kindled for him from helmet and shield an untiring fire,
like the star of late summer, which most of all
brightly shines, having bathed in Oceanus;

for not even he himself would have escaped the black death-fate,
but Hephaestus guarded him, and saved him, having covered him in night,
so that the old man might not be wholly grieved.

And the great-hearted Trojans, when they saw the two sons of Dares,
the one having got away, the other slain beside the chariot,
in all was stirred the spirit;

for in the back, as he was first to turn, the spear he fixed,
between the shoulders, and through the chest he drove it,
and he fell with a thud, and his armour clattered upon him.

And the son of Strophius, Scamandrius, skilled in the hunt,
Atreus’-son Menelaus took with his sharp spear,
a good hunter;

And Meriones slew Phereclus, the son of the builder
Harmonides, who with his hands knew all intricate works
to make;

him then Eurypylus, Euaemon’s splendid son,
before himself as he fled, running after, struck on the shoulder
with his sword, darting, and hewed off the heavy hand;

and it neither do the bridges, hemmed in, hold back,
nor do the fences of the flourishing orchards check,
when it comes suddenly, when the rain of Zeus bears down heavily;
